## Development Patterns That Work

### Model Configuration Patterns
```cpp
// Whisper - Working Configuration
config.model_path = "ggml-large-v3-turbo-q8_0.bin";
config.language = "ja";
config.segment_length_ms = 4000;
config.overlap_ms = 750;
config.vad_threshold = 0.5f;

// Llama - Target Configuration
config.n_gpu_layers = 99;  // Use all GPU layers
config.n_ctx = 2048;       // Context window
sampler: greedy;           // Deterministic output
```

### Threading Patterns
- Main Thread: UI and event handling only
- Audio Thread: PortAudio callback, minimal processing
- AI Threads: Separate for transcription and translation
- Use std::atomic<bool> for thread-safe status flags

### Error Handling Strategy
- AI model failures should gracefully degrade (continue with single model)
- GPU OOM should fallback to CPU processing
- Always check Initialize() return values
- Use std::cerr for error logging, std::cout for status

## Code Quality Patterns

### Memory Management
- Use RAII for all AI resources
- std::optional<> for conditional heavy objects
- Fixed-size buffers for audio (prevent memory growth)
- std::jthread for automatic thread cleanup

### Performance Optimization
- Process audio in 4-second segments with 750ms overlap
- Use quantized models (Q4_K_M) for memory efficiency
- Lock-free queues for audio pipeline
- GPU acceleration critical for real-time performance

## File Organization Intelligence

### Key Files to Understand
- `TranscribeLayer.hpp`: Main orchestrator, start here
- `WhisperTranscriber.cpp`: Working AI integration example
- `MarinaTranslator.cpp`: Incomplete, needs inference implementation
- `Core/Stream/`: Lock-free audio streaming system
- `Core/Application.hpp`: Layer management framework

### Build System Notes
- vcpkg.json: All dependencies properly configured
- CMake presets: Use `cmake --preset vcpkg`
- Git submodules: whisper.cpp and llama.cpp (may need sync)
- CUDA required for optimal performance

## Japanese Language Specifics

### Whisper Configuration for Japanese
- Use large-v3-turbo model for best accuracy
- Segment length 4000ms (longer for Japanese processing)
- VAD threshold 0.5 (lower for Japanese speech patterns)
- Always set language="ja" explicitly

### Translation Considerations
- Japanese→English requires context awareness
- Gemma-2-2b model chosen for Japanese translation
- Need proper prompt engineering in Generate() method
- Consider cultural context in translation quality

## Development Workflow

### When Adding Features
1. Always check Memory Bank files first (memory-bank/*.md)
2. Follow existing patterns in Core/ and App/Modules/
3. Add new UI components following TranscribeUIComponent pattern
4. Test with real Japanese audio samples
5. Verify GPU memory usage with both models loaded

### When Debugging
- Check atomic flags first (isTranscribing, isRecording, etc.)
- GPU issues: Check CUDA availability and memory
- Audio issues: Verify WASAPI device compatibility
- UI issues: Check ImGui context and docking state

### Performance Testing
- Test with 30+ minute recordings
- Monitor GPU memory usage during dual processing
- Verify <3 second latency for transcription
- Test CPU fallback when GPU unavailable

## Integration Wisdom

### What Works Well Together
- PortAudio + WASAPI for low-latency audio
- ImGui + GLFW for responsive UI
- std::jthread + atomic flags for threading
- SoXR for high-quality audio resampling

### What to Avoid
- Don't mix raw pointers with RAII objects
- Don't block UI thread with model loading
- Don't share mutable state between threads
- Don't ignore AI model initialization failures

This project is 75% complete with excellent foundations. The core challenge is completing the Llama translation inference to achieve the full vision of real-time Japanese transcription with English translation. 